Expose password-protected external recipients and contact writes (Hylki)

Confidential mail to a recipient outside Tuta goes through an external user
the sender's account owns, unlocked by a passphrase shared out of band. TS
builds it in MailFacade.addRecipientKeyData (getExternalGroupKeys,
createExternalUser); the SDK has the entities and the ExternalUserService but
no code for it, and the KeyLoaderFacade and UserFacade it needs are private.

This adds crypto::secure_external_facade::SecureExternalFacade, a port of
that arm: an Argon2id passphrase key over a fresh 16-byte salt, the verifier
sha256(key), saltHash sha256(salt), the external user looked up through
GroupRoot.externalUserReferences (keyed by the trimmed, lower-cased address)
and its group keys unwrapped with the user group key at the recorded admin
version (KeyLoaderFacade.load_sym_group_key), or a new external user posted
to the ExternalUserService with fresh keys wrapped as TS wraps them; then
ownerEncBucketKey under the external mail group key and
pwEncCommunicationKey under the passphrase key. An external sender, a shared
mailbox and any recipient without a passphrase are refused before anything
is loaded, as in TS. LoggedInSdk keeps the facade and exposes the call.

ContactFacade gains create_contact (a new contact in the user's contact list,
under a fresh session key wrapped with the contact group key, as TS
EntityClient.setup with the AES-CBC scheme) and set_preshared_password (a
PATCH of presharedPassword alone under the contact's session key, as TS
SendMailModel.updateContacts writes it); an AEAD contact is refused, since
the SDK writes no AEAD values. EntityClient.create_instance returns the
server's HttpError for a non-2xx status instead of panicking. No new
cryptography: only existing SDK primitives are composed.

